WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.500
Expert performance is domain-specific.

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:06.420
The conditions that define strong performance in technical work are not identical to the

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:07.870
conditions in operations,

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:12.000
incident response, leadership, communication, creative production, or physical skill.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.000
Each domain has its own cues, constraints, risks, standards, and patterns of failure.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.775
A person can be excellent in one environment and still need practice, context,

00:00:19.775 --> 00:00:20.965
or support in another.

00:00:20.965 --> 00:00:25.000
Real-world conditions matter because performance is never separate from context.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:26.950
Time pressure changes how much analysis is possible.

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:29.975
Incomplete information forces judgment under uncertainty.

00:00:29.975 --> 00:00:30.956
Interruptions break attention.

00:00:30.956 --> 00:00:33.939
Tradeoffs require deciding what matters most right now.

00:00:33.939 --> 00:00:36.090
A clean classroom example may reveal understanding,

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:39.500
but it does not fully prove performance in the environment where the skill will actually

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:40.000
be used.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:42.985
This is why performance should be evaluated in context.

00:00:42.985 --> 00:00:45.810
A strong incident responder needs to prioritize, communicate,

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:47.965
and update assumptions during an active event.

00:00:47.965 --> 00:00:51.775
A strong engineer needs to make sound decisions within architecture, cost,

00:00:51.775 --> 00:00:54.000
maintainability, and risk constraints.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.975
A strong leader needs to create clarity when people are under pressure.

00:00:56.975 --> 00:01:01.000
The performance standard has to reflect the actual job, not an idealized version of it.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.975
Training and assessment should therefore include realistic constraints.

00:01:03.975 --> 00:01:06.985
That does not mean every exercise must be high stakes.

00:01:06.985 --> 00:01:11.000
It means practice should gradually include the conditions that shape real performance:

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.040
limited time, partial evidence, handoffs, interruptions, changing priorities,

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:16.000
and consequences.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.480
The closer the practice gets to meaningful conditions,

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.930
the more likely the skill will transfer back to the work.
